    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d65">

                <pb facs="0065" n="55" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg55"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Madame Chiang Kai-shek
                        to Mr. Lauchlin Currie
                    </hi><anchor n="12" xml:id="d65-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-05-23">May 23, 1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Morale of Army and people never lower during five years of war than since fall of
                    Burma. Three divisions escaped Japanese encirclement north of Myitkyina, but
                    many amongst them died of starvation. Reactionaries here actively raising ugly
                    heads, making head way with anti-war propaganda. For first time since 1937,
                    Chief is pessimistic, admits situation dangerous. This for your confidential
                    information because future is extremely critical. Magruder is taking you
                    important letter.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d65-f1" n="12" type="source" xml:id="d65-f1-content">
                <p>Copy of telegram transmitted to President Roosevelt. Photostatic copy obtained
                    from the Franklin D. Roosevelt Library, Hyde Park, N. Y.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
